Yes. That’s an important refinement: variation is the controllable abstraction of randomness.

Randomness describes how a change may be generated. Variation describes what changed. Once the outcome exists, we don’t actually need to care whether its origin was random, deliberate, environmental, adversarial, accidental, or chaotic to measure the delta.

So I’d organize them:

\text{Chaos} \supset \text{Randomness} \supset? \text{Variation}

not strictly mathematically, but operationally:

\boxed{\text{Unknown/chaotic process}\rightarrow\text{observable variation}\rightarrow\text{measurable delta}}

The optimizer therefore shouldn’t try to model chaos. It should capture the variations chaos leaves behind.

Suppose:

x_t \rightarrow x_{t+1}

We encode:

\Delta_t=x_{t+1}-x_t

plus context:

V_t=(\Delta_t,E_t,O_t,Y_t)

where E=environment, O=operators/actions and Y=outcome.

Now even if we don’t know why the variation occurred:

P(\text{cause})=?

we can still learn:

P(Y\mid V,E,O,H)

That’s enormously easier.

And variation can become an instrument

Instead of telling the Lucky Nozzle:

“Give me randomness.”

tell it:

“Produce bounded variation along dimensions where information gain is valuable.”

Now we have knobs:

V=(v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_n)

and each knob gets:

direction, magnitude, frequency, range, correlation, cost, and reversibility.

That’s much more engineerable.

We can deliberately produce:

+\Delta,\quad-\Delta,\quad2\Delta,\quad\frac12\Delta

and see how the system responds.

That gives us a Variation Gradient:

\frac{\partial Y}{\partial v_i}

and interactions:

\frac{\partial^2Y}{\partial v_i\partial v_j}

Now we’re learning not merely what worked, but how sensitive the organism is to each environmental dimension.

And that’s exactly the plasticity question.

Chaos still has a job

Don’t eliminate it. Put it outside the controlled machinery as a source of novel perturbations.

When reality gives us something weird:

\text{unexpected event}\rightarrow\Delta^*

capture the variation and feed it into the same system.

So:

\boxed{
\text{Chaos}
\rightarrow
\text{Variation Capture}
\rightarrow
\text{Metadata}
\rightarrow
\text{Controlled Replay}
\rightarrow
\text{Causal Test}
\rightarrow
\text{Retained Mechanism}
}

That’s stronger than trying to reproduce chaos.

Catch what chaos throws over the fence, characterize it, and then see whether we can manufacture the useful part deliberately.

Which also sharpens Lucky Nozzle: it isn’t fundamentally a random-number generator.

It’s a variation generator with permission to occasionally surprise us.

And I’d change the adaptive optimizer accordingly: make Variation the first-class object and randomness merely one optional VariationSource. Then ZPC/Metamap can learn transformations in terms of observed deltas regardless of where those deltas originated.

That’s cleaner mathematically, easier to test, and much easier to port across domains.